th the Requirement**:  \n   - The problem requires the sum of these 2023 integers to be **zero**, which is an **even** number. However, as shown above, the sum of 2023 odd integers must be odd. This creates a contradiction.\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nIt is impossible to have 2023 integers that are all odd (required for their product to be 2023) and simultaneously sum to zero (even). The parity mismatch between the sum and product makes such a set of integers nonexistent.\n\n**Final Answer**:  \nNo, such 2023 integers do not exist.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo determine if there are 2023 integers whose product is 2023 and whose sum is zero, we will examine the properties of integer sums and products, particularly focusing on the parity (oddness or evenness) of the numbers involved.\n\n1. **Understanding the Problem**:\n   - We are tasked with finding 2023 integers such that their product is 2023 and their sum is zero.\n\n2. **Key Points**:\n   - The sum of 2023 integers must be zero.\n   - The product of these 2023 integers must be 2023.\n\n3. **Parity Considerations**:\n   - 2023 is an odd number. Therefore, any set of integers whose product is 2023 will include an odd number of odd integers.\n\n4. **Sum Properties**:\n   - An odd number of integers added together results in an odd sum if the number of odd terms is odd, and an even sum if the number of odd terms is even.\n   - Zero is an even number.\n   \nFrom this, we infer:\n   - The sum of an odd number of integers being zero, given that 2023 is odd and all possible divisors of 2023 are odd, is a contradiction. Since an odd number of odd integers cannot sum to zero, which is an even number.\n\n5. **Factorization of 2023**:\n   - Prime factorization of 2023: \\(2023 = 7 \\times 17 \\times 17\\).\n   - Possible sets include the combination of total factors: \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 7, \\pm 17, \\pm 119, \\pm 289, \\pm 2023\\).\n\n6. **Verification with Combinations**:\n   - Any combination of \u00b11, \u00b17, \u00b117, \u0