o we need to solve for t and s such that:\n\n-r + t(-h + r) = h + s(r - h)\n\nand\n\nt k = k - s k.\n\nFrom the second equation: t k = k(1 - s). Dividing both sides by k (assuming k \u2260 0, which it must be since QB is parallel to PA and not coinciding, so k \u2260 0), we get t = 1 - s.\n\nNow substitute t = 1 - s into the first equation:\n\n-r + (1 - s)(-h + r) = h + s(r - h)\n\nLet me expand the left side:\n\n-r + (-h + r)(1 - s) = -r + (-h + r) - (-h + r)s = -r - h + r - (-h s + r s) = -h - (-h s + r s) = -h + h s - r s\n\nRight side: h + s(r - h) = h + r s - h s\n\nSo set left side equal to right side:\n\n-h + h s - r s = h + r s - h s\n\nBring all terms to the left side:\n\n-h + h s - r s - h - r s + h s = 0\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(-h - h) + (h s + h s) + (-r s - r s) = 0\n\n-2h + 2h s - 2r s = 0\n\nFactor:\n\n-2h + 2s(h - r) = 0\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n-h + s(h - r) = 0\n\nSolve for s:\n\ns(h - r) = h\n\ns = h / (h - r)\n\nBut h is a coordinate such that h\u00b2 + k\u00b2 = r\u00b2. So h = sqrt(r\u00b2 - k\u00b2). Hmm, but maybe we can express s in terms of h and r.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a better approach.\n\nWait, maybe using coordinate geometry here is getting a bit messy. Let me think if there's a more geometric approach.\n\nSince PORS is a parallelogram, then vector OR is equal to vector PS, and vector OS is equal to vector PR. But maybe using vectors could simplify things.\n\nLet me consider vectors. Let O be the origin. Then vectors OP = P - O = P, since O is the origin. Similarly for other points.\n\nSince PORS is a parallelogram, the vector OS = OP + OR. Wait, in a parallelogram, the position vectors satisfy S = P + R - O. Wait, maybe.\n\nWait, in a parallelogram P-O-R-S, the sides PO and OR are adjacent sides. So to get from O to R, you go from O to R directly. To get from P to S, you go the same vector as from O to R. So vector PS = vector OR. Wait, maybe that's not quite right.\n\nAlternatively, in a parallelogram, the diagonals bisect each other. But perhaps using vectors more carefully.\n\nIf PORS is a parallelogram, then the vector OR is equal to the vector PS, and the vector OP is equal to the vector RS. Wait, perhaps:\n\nIn a parallelogram, opposite sides are equal and parallel. So PO is equal and parallel to RS, and OR is equal and parallel to PS.\n\nTherefore, RS = PO. Since PO is the vector from P to O, which is O - P. So RS = O - P. Similarly, PS = OR.\n\nBut S is a point such that PORS is a parallelogram. So starting at P, moving along the vector OR gets you to S. So S = P + OR. But OR is the vector from O to R, which is R - O = R. So S = P + R. Wait, but since O is the origin, R is just the position vector of R. So S = P + R.\n\nWait, if O is the origin, then in the parallelogram P-O-R-S, the coordinates of S would be O + (R - P). Wait, maybe not. Let me think again.\n\nIn a parallelogram, the fourth vertex can be found by adding the vectors of the adjacent sides. So starting at P, moving along OR (from O to R) would take you to S. So S = P + (R - O) = P + R, since O is the origin.\n\nAlternatively, since O is the origin, the coordinates of S can be found as O + (R - P) = R - P. Wait, maybe not. Let me check with vectors.\n\nIf we have a parallelogram with vertices P, O, R, S, then the sides PO and OR are vectors from P to O and from O to R. Then the fourth vertex S can be obtained by starting at P and moving along the vector OR, which is S = P + OR. Since OR is the vector from O to R, which is R (since O is origin). So S = P + R.\n\nAlternatively, starting at O and moving along PR? No, maybe not. Let me confirm.\n\nIn a parallelogram, the vector from P to O is equal to the vector from R to S. So PO = RS. Similarly, the vector from O to R is equal to the vector from P to S. So OR = PS.\n\nTherefore, since OR = PS, then S = P + OR. Since OR is the vector from O to R, which is just R (since O is origin). So S = P + R.\n\nSimilarly, since PO = RS, then RS = PO = O - P. Therefore,