iven that QB is parallel to PA, which is a diameter, and O is the center. Maybe there's some symmetry here. Since QB is parallel to PA, the line QB is horizontal if PA is horizontal. Then, since PA is a diameter, A is the point diametrically opposite P.\n\nSince QB is parallel to PA, and QB is a chord, then by the properties of circles, the line QB is at some distance from PA. Then, lines PB and QA intersect at R. Maybe using similar triangles or something.\n\nAlternatively, since QB is parallel to PA, then the angles subtended by these chords might be related. Alternatively, since PA is a diameter, the angle PQA is a right angle (since any angle subtended by a diameter is a right angle). Wait, but Q is on the circle, so angle PQA would be 90 degrees. Wait, point Q is on the circle, PA is the diameter, so yes, angle PQA is 90 degrees. Therefore, QA is perpendicular to QP. Wait, QA is from Q to A, and QP is from Q to P. Since PA is the diameter, angle PQA is 90 degrees. Therefore, triangle PQA is right-angled at Q.\n\nSimilarly, since QB is parallel to PA, which is horizontal, then QB is horizontal. Then, line PB connects P to B, which is a point on QB. Since QB is horizontal, and PA is horizontal, and O is the center, perhaps there's some reflection symmetry.\n\nAlternatively, consider coordinates again. Let me assign coordinates with O at (0,0), PA as the x-axis, P at (-r, 0), A at (r, 0). QB is a horizontal chord at y = k, so Q is (h, k) and B is (-h, k), since QB is parallel to PA and hence horizontal. Then, since Q and B are on the circle, h\u00b2 + k\u00b2 = r\u00b2.\n\nEquation of line PB: connects (-r, 0) to (-h, k). The slope is (k - 0)/(-h + r) = k/(r - h). So equation is y = [k/(r - h)](x + r).\n\nEquation of line QA: connects (h, k) to (r, 0). The slope is (0 - k)/(r - h) = -k/(r - h). Equation is y - k = [-k/(r - h)](x - h).\n\nFind intersection R of these two lines.\n\nFrom line PB: y = [k/(r - h)](x + r).\n\nFrom line QA: y = [-k/(r - h)](x - h) + k.\n\nSet equal:\n\n[k/(r - h)](x + r) = [-k/(r - h)](x - h) + k.\n\nMultiply both sides by (r - h)/k:\n\nx + r = - (x - h) + (r - h).\n\nSimplify right side: -x + h + r - h = -x + r.\n\nSo equation: x + r = -x + r.\n\nBring -x to left: 2x + r = r \u21d2 2x = 0 \u21d2 x = 0.\n\nThen y = [k/(r - h)](0 + r) = [k/(r - h)] * r = (kr)/(r - h).\n\nTherefore, point R has coordinates (0, kr/(r - h)).\n\nNow, S is such that PORS is a parallelogram. As per earlier, if S is determined by the midpoint of diagonals PR and OS, then S = P + R.\n\nGiven P is (-r, 0) and R is (0, kr/(r - h)), then S = (-r, 0) + (0, kr/(r - h)) = (-r, kr/(r - h)).\n\nWait, but according to the earlier midpoint consideration, if diagonals PR and OS bisect each other, then S = P + R. So in coordinates, S would be (-r + 0, 0 + kr/(r - h)) = (-r, kr/(r - h)).\n\nNow, we need to compute SP and SQ.\n\nFirst, SP is the distance from S to P. S is (-r, kr/(r - h)), P is (-r, 0). Therefore, SP = sqrt[(-r + r)^2 + (kr/(r - h) - 0)^2] = sqrt[0 + (kr/(r - h))^2] = |kr/(r - h)|.\n\nSince distances are positive, SP = kr/(r - h). Note that r > h because h\u00b2 + k\u00b2 = r\u00b2, so h < r (assuming k \u2260 0). Therefore, r - h is positive, so SP = kr/(r - h).\n\nNext, SQ is the distance from S to Q. Q is (h, k). S is (-r, kr/(r - h)).\n\nTherefore, SQ = sqrt[(h + r)^2 + (k - kr/(r - h))^2].\n\nLet's compute this:\n\nFirst, the x-coordinate difference: h + r.\n\nThe y-coordinate difference: k - (kr)/(r - h) = k[1 - r/(r - h)] = k[(r - h - r)/(r - h)] = k[-h/(r - h)] = -kh/(r - h).\n\nTherefore, SQ = sqrt[(h + r)^2 + (-kh/(r - h))^2] = sqrt[(r + h)^2 + (k\u00b2 h\u00b2)/(r - h)^2].\n\nFactor out 1/(r - h)^2 from both terms:\n\n= sqrt[ ( (r + h)^2 (r - h)^2 + k\u00b2 h\u00b2 ) / (r - h)^2 ) ]\n\n= sqrt[ ( (r\u00b2 - h\u00b2)^2 + k\u00b2 h\u00b2 ) / (r - h)^2 ) ]\n\nBut since h\u00b2 + k\u00b2 = r\u00b2, we can substitute k\u00b2 = r\u00b2 - h\u00b2.\n\nTherefore, the numerator becomes:\n\n(r\u00b2 - h\u00b2)^2 + (r\u00b