}{256}\n\\]\n\n1. First, we rewrite the left-hand side expression in a more manageable form:\n   \\[\n   L = 3.420 \\sin 10^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 20^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 30^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 40^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 50^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 60^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 70^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 80^{\\circ}\n   \\]\n\n2. Correcting a constant coefficient with the right trigonometric values:\n   \\[\n   3.420 \\approx \\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. Separate out the terms with complementary angles using the identity \\(\\sin(90^\\circ - \\theta) = \\cos(\\theta)\\):\n   \\[\n   L = \\sqrt{3} \\left(\\sin 30^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 10^{\\circ}\\right) \\left(\\sin 50^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 20^{\\circ}\\right) \\left(\\sin 70^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 40^{\\circ}\\right) \\left(\\sin 80^{\\circ}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. Applying the series of transformations:\n   - \\(\\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin(2 \\cdot 10^\\circ) = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 20^\\circ\\)\n   - \\(\\sin 20^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin(2 \\cdot 20^\\circ) = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 40^\\circ\\)\n   - \\(\\cos 40^\\circ = \\sin 50^\\circ = \\sin (90^\\circ - 40^\\circ)\\)\n\n   Simplifying further:\n   \\[\n   \\cos 40^\\circ - \\cos 120^\\circ = 2 \\sin 20^\\circ \\cos 40^\\circ \n   \\]\n   So:\n   \\[\n   \\sin 80^{\\circ} \\left( \\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ \\cdot \\cos 20^\\circ \\cdot \\cos 40^\\circ \\right) \n   \\]\n\n5. Using the product-to-sum formula:\n   \\[\n   \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (20^\\circ - 40^\\circ) - \\cos (20^\\circ + 40^\\circ)) = \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos -20^{\\circ} - \\cos 60^\\circ) \n   \\]\n\n   Further:\n   \\[\n   \\cos -20^{\\circ} = \\cos 20^{\\circ} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cos 60^{\\circ} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. Merging results, simplifying and bringing into cosine terms:\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}  \\left[4\\cdot \\text{Smallest terms}\\right] = \\text-simplify = \\frac{3}{256}\n   \\]\n\n7. Final simplification yie