nce z must be \u22652x +4y and \u22642 -x -y, the region exists only where2x +4y <2 -x -y \u21d23x +5y <2. Therefore, V_upper is:\n\nV_upper = \u222b\u222b_{3x +5y <2} \u222b_{2x +4y}^{2 -x -y} dz dy dx\n\nBut wait, in this region, z ranges from2x +4y to2 -x -y. However, since3x +5y <2, we have2 -x -y >2x +4y.\n\nTherefore,\n\nV_upper = \u222b\u222b_{3x +5y <2} [ (2 -x -y) - (2x +4y) ] dy dx\n\n= \u222b\u222b_{3x +5y <2} (2 -x -y -2x -4y) dy dx\n\n= \u222b\u222b_{3x +5y <2} (2 -3x -5y) dy dx\n\nThis integral is over the region3x +5y <2, x \u22650, y \u22650.\n\nThis is the same region as I1 but integrating (2 -3x -5y) instead of (2x +4y).\n\nLet's compute this integral:\n\nV_upper = \u222b_{x=0}^{2/3} \u222b_{y=0}^{(2 -3x)/5} (2 -3x -5y) dy dx\n\nFirst, compute the inner integral:\n\n\u222b_{0}^{(2 -3x)/5} (2 -3x -5y) dy\n\n= (2 -3x)y - (5/2)y\u00b2 evaluated from0 to(2 -3x)/5\n\nAt upper limit:\n\n(2 -3x)*( (2 -3x)/5 ) - (5/2)*( (2 -3x)^2 /25 )\n\n= ( (2 -3x)^2 )/5 - ( (2 -3x)^2 )/10\n\n= (2*(2 -3x)^2 - (2 -3x)^2 )/10\n\n= ( (2 -3x)^2 )/10\n\nTherefore, V_upper = \u222b_{0}^{2/3} ( (2 -3x)^2 )/10 dx\n\nLet me compute this:\n\n= (1/10) \u222b_{0}^{2/3} (4 -12x +9x\u00b2) dx\n\n= (1/10)[4x -6x\u00b2 +3x\u00b3] from0 to2/3\n\nAt x=2/3:\n\n4*(2/3) -6*(4/9) +3*(8/27)\n\n=8/3 -24/9 +24/27\n\nConvert to common denominator27:\n\n=72/27 -72/27 +24/27\n\n=24/27 =8/9\n\nAt x=0, all terms are0.\n\nTherefore, V_upper = (1/10)*(8/9) =8/90=4/45\u22480.0889\n\nThis matches the previous result where V_upper=4/45 and V_lower=56/45, totaling4/3.\n\nTherefore, the volume ratio is V_lower : V_upper=56/45 :4/45=14:1.\n\nHowever, the problem asks, \"In what ratio does this plane divide the volume of the pyramid?\" It's customary to present the ratio as the smaller part to the larger part, or the parts in order. Since V_lower is14 parts and V_upper is1 part, the ratio is14:1.\n\nBut according to the problem statement, the plane passes through A, bisects the median SK, and intersects SL at