C goes from (0,0) to (6,6), and line p is parallel to that (slope 1) passing through D (0,6). So the equation of line p would be y = x + 6, since it has slope 1 and passes through (0,6). Wait, because if you plug x=0 into y = x + 6, you get y=6, which is point D.\n\nNow, rectangle ACEF. The vertices A and C are already part of the square. E and F are on line p. So rectangle ACEF must have sides AC and CE (or AF, depending on the configuration). Wait, how exactly is the rectangle arranged? Let me think. Since AC is a diagonal of the square, which is 6\u221a2 cm long. If ACEF is a rectangle, then sides AC and CE must be adjacent sides of the rectangle. Wait, but AC is a diagonal, so in the rectangle, AC would be one side, but rectangles have sides that are perpendicular. Wait, that can't be right. Wait, no, in a rectangle, the sides are not necessarily perpendicular to the diagonals. Wait, actually, a rectangle has four right angles. So if AC is a side of the rectangle, then the adjacent sides must be perpendicular to AC. But AC in the square is a diagonal, so it's at 45 degrees. If we need a rectangle with AC as one side, then the other sides would be perpendicular to AC. But in this case, the problem says the rectangle is ACEF with E and F on line p. Hmm, maybe I need to clarify the structure.\n\nWait, perhaps the rectangle is constructed such that AC is one of its diagonals? Wait, but the problem says \"rectangle ACEF\". So the vertices are A, C, E, F. So the order is important. So the rectangle is A connected to C connected to E connected to F connected back to A? But in a rectangle, the vertices should be connected such that the sides are AC, CE, EF, FA. But AC is already a diagonal of the square. Wait, maybe not. Let me try to parse the problem again.\n\n\"Construct a rectangle AC E F such that vertices E and F lie on the line p.\" So the rectangle is named ACEF, which probably means the vertices are A, C, E, F connected in that order. But rectangles have their 