ore, the key idea is that the sequence cannot cover all primes. \n\nTo formalize this, assume for contradiction that every prime divides some term of the sequence. Then, consider the product P of all primes. However, P is not a finite product, but in our case, the sequence terms are constructed by adding a to the product of previous terms. If every prime divides some term, then for each prime p, there exists a term e\u2099 such that p divides e\u2099. Let p be a prime greater than a. Since e\u2099 =a + product of previous terms, and product of previous terms is at least 1 (since e\u2080=1), so e\u2099 \u2265a +1. If p divides e\u2099, then e\u2099 \u2265p. But primes can be arbitrarily large, so for each prime p, there exists e\u2099 \u2265p such that p divides e\u2099. However, by Euclid's theorem, there are infinitely many primes, so the sequence would have to generate numbers divisible by arbitrarily large primes. However, the sequence grows rapidly, but each term is a + product of previous terms. The product grows faster than exponentially, so the terms e\u2099 are certainly large enough to include all primes, but the question is whether the structure of e\u2099 allows every prime to divide some term. \n\nSuppose p is a prime greater than a. If p divides e\u2099 for some n, then e\u2099 \u22610 mod p. Therefore, product_{k=0}^{n-1} e_k \u2261 -a mod p. But the product is congruent to some number modulo p, so -a \u2261 product mod p. However, since p >a, -a is not congruent to 0 mod p. Therefore, the product must be \u2261 -a mod p. This implies that the product of the previous terms is \u2261 -a mod p. However, the previous terms are e\u2080=1, e\u2081, e\u2082,..., e_{n-1}. The product of these terms mod p is some value, and we need it to be \u2261 -a mod p. But there are only finitely many possible products modulo p (specifically, (p-1)^{n} possibilities for n terms), but since p is arbitrary large, the probability that one of these products equals -a mod p de