re exists at least one prime not dividing any term of the sequence. \n\nSo, summarizing:\n\n(a) By showing that each term is coprime to all previous terms, hence introducing new prime factors each time, implying infinitely many primes divide the sequence.\n\n(b) By considering primes dividing a (if a>1) or explicitly finding a prime like 5 (if a=1), which does not divide any term.", "deepseek_solution": "**Solution:**\n\n**(a)**  \nWe observe that each term \\( e_n \\) in the sequence is defined as \\( e_n = a + \\prod_{k=0}^{n-1} e_k \\). For \\( n \\geq 1 \\), \\( e_n \\) is constructed by adding \\( a \\) to the product of all previous terms. \n\n**Key Steps:**\n1. **Coprimality:** For \\( n \\geq 1 \\), \\( e_n \\) is coprime to all previous terms \\( e_0, e_1, \\ldots, e_{n-1} \\). This is because if a prime \\( p \\) divides \\( e_n \\) and some \\( e_m \\) (where \\( m < n \\)), then \\( p \\) must divide \\( a \\). However, \\( e_1 = a + 1 \\equiv 1 \\mod p \\), leading to a contradiction.\n2. **New Prime Factors:** Since each \\( e_n \\) is coprime to all prior terms, it must contain at least one new prime factor not present in any earlier term. As the sequence is infinite, this implies infinitely many distinct primes divide the sequence.\n\nThus, there are infinitely many primes that divide some element of the sequence.\n\n**(b)**  \nWe consider two cases based on the value of \\( a \\):\n\n1. **Case \\( a > 1 \\):**  \n   Let \\( p \\) be any prime divisor of \\( a \\). For all \\( n \\geq 1 \\), \\( e_n \\equiv 1 \\mod p \\). This is shown inductively:\n   - \\( e_1 = a + 1 \\equiv 1 \\mod p \\),\n   - Subsequent terms \\( e_n = a + \\prod_{k=0}^{n-1} e_k \\equiv 0 + \\prod_{k=0}^{n-1} 1 \\equiv 1 \\mod p \\).\n   Hence, \\( p \\) does not divide any term in the sequence.\n\n2. **Case \\( a = 1 \\):**  \n   The sequence becomes \\( 1, 2, 3, 7, 43, \\ldots \\). Consider the prime \\( p = 5 \\):\n   - Modulo 5, the sequence cycles between \\( 2, 3, 2, 3, \